WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.083 --> 00:00:01.501
- [Dotson:] The one thing about me,

00:00:01.501 --> 00:00:03.378
you don't have to coach my effort.

00:00:03.378 --> 00:00:05.547
Ima give 110 percent when I step on the court.

00:00:05.547 --> 00:00:06.423
[hip-hop music]

00:00:06.423 --> 00:00:09.134
- [Announcer 1:] Spin move by Dotson!

00:00:09.134 --> 00:00:10.760
- [Announcer 2:] Are you serious?

00:00:10.760 --> 00:00:11.594
- [Announcer 1:] Dotson...

00:00:11.594 --> 00:00:12.429
[whistle chirps]

00:00:12.429 --> 00:00:13.805
[crowd cheers]

00:00:13.805 --> 00:00:15.765
- [Announcer 1:] Dotson
gets a screen. Dotson!

00:00:15.765 --> 00:00:19.394
- [Dotson:] I feel like the
reason why I'm entering the draft now,

00:00:19.394 --> 00:00:22.022
um...the time is right, you know.
What I showed this past season,

00:00:22.022 --> 00:00:24.232
talked to my coaching
staff, my family, and,

00:00:25.066 --> 00:00:27.193
you know, I'm ready for
that next challenge.

00:00:27.193 --> 00:00:28.403
That next step in my life.

00:00:28.403 --> 00:00:30.697
- The longest draft process ever, right?

00:00:30.697 --> 00:00:32.198
You know, I think we all know that.

00:00:32.198 --> 00:00:33.575
But, you know, all in all,

00:00:33.575 --> 00:00:35.035
it's been really good for him, I think.

00:00:35.035 --> 00:00:37.287
This is probably the biggest gap of time

00:00:37.287 --> 00:00:40.290
when he's really been
able to focus on himself

00:00:40.290 --> 00:00:41.750
and his individual skill.

00:00:41.750 --> 00:00:44.252
We're all anxious. We're
ready for November 18.

00:00:44.252 --> 00:00:46.171
But I think it has been good

00:00:46.171 --> 00:00:49.966
in that he's been able to work
on himself and get better.

00:00:49.966 --> 00:00:52.343
- He's had to
endure a lot of patience,

00:00:52.343 --> 00:00:54.262
a lot of flexibility,

00:00:54.262 --> 00:00:57.098
but you know what, it's just
going to make him tougher

00:00:57.098 --> 00:01:00.477
and that much more of a
pro at the next level.

00:01:00.477 --> 00:01:03.271
[crowd cheering]

00:01:03.271 --> 00:01:04.105
- [Announcer 1:] Dotson.

00:01:04.105 --> 00:01:05.857
Yes!

00:01:06.816 --> 00:01:08.109
- [Announcer 1:] Dotson leans in.

00:01:08.109 --> 00:01:08.985
Yes!

00:01:08.985 --> 00:01:12.155
- Looking back on this Kansas
year, it was special.

00:01:12.155 --> 00:01:15.075
We're number one in the nation
for three straight weeks.

00:01:15.075 --> 00:01:17.118
We won the conference title.

00:01:17.118 --> 00:01:19.120
That was our goal coming into the season.

00:01:19.120 --> 00:01:21.331
- [Announcer 2:] Oh boy!
Dotson for three.

00:01:21.331 --> 00:01:22.832
- [Dotson:] That late-game success,

00:01:22.832 --> 00:01:25.627
I think it's just a natural thing

00:01:25.627 --> 00:01:28.630
that I've always had since I was little.

00:01:28.630 --> 00:01:30.548
I never wanted to lose in anything.

00:01:30.548 --> 00:01:32.592
It could be a card game, Uno game.

00:01:32.592 --> 00:01:35.595
I'm just never trying to lose.

00:01:35.595 --> 00:01:36.846
- We played in the basement a lot.

00:01:36.846 --> 00:01:39.140
And sometimes we'll play one-on-one,

00:01:39.140 --> 00:01:40.767
and we get kind of rough

00:01:40.767 --> 00:01:41.935
cuz, you know, we're pretty physical.

00:01:41.935 --> 00:01:42.936
We both hated to lose.

00:01:42.936 --> 00:01:46.022
So, we would kind of go
back and forth, get mad,

00:01:46.022 --> 00:01:47.857
cuz one person fouled
each other too hard.

00:01:47.857 --> 00:01:51.319
- I went to
the same school as him

00:01:51.319 --> 00:01:52.570
when he was in middle school.

00:01:52.570 --> 00:01:54.906
And he was, like, the star, you know,

00:01:54.906 --> 00:01:56.157
of the basketball team.

00:01:56.157 --> 00:01:57.617
That's when he started taking it seriously

00:01:57.617 --> 00:01:59.744
cuz he was really good
at such a young age.

00:01:59.744 --> 00:02:03.414
- Around sixth grade,
my older brother, actually, was

00:02:03.414 --> 00:02:05.250
the reason why I got serious with it.

00:02:05.250 --> 00:02:07.710
He's three years older than me,

00:02:07.710 --> 00:02:09.963
and that's when he started
to get serious with it,

00:02:09.963 --> 00:02:11.798
so I tried to follow in his footsteps.

00:02:11.798 --> 00:02:14.175
And that's when things started to pick up,

00:02:14.175 --> 00:02:17.303
started to see my game advance.

00:02:17.303 --> 00:02:19.180
- He grew up watching Derrick Rose,

00:02:19.180 --> 00:02:22.684
so that's kind of been the
person that he's watched

00:02:22.684 --> 00:02:25.311
at an early age. And,

00:02:25.311 --> 00:02:26.813
you know, he modeled a lot of his game

00:02:26.813 --> 00:02:28.148
because he had quickness,

00:02:28.148 --> 00:02:31.317
he had some explosiveness like Derrick,

00:02:32.277 --> 00:02:33.945
how Derrick likes to attack the basket

00:02:33.945 --> 00:02:35.238
and attack in transition.

00:02:35.238 --> 00:02:37.115
And, you know, those are
the two biggest strengths

00:02:37.115 --> 00:02:38.992
of, of Devon, is just
being able to beat his man

00:02:38.992 --> 00:02:41.536
off the dribble and to
push tempo in transition.

00:02:41.536 --> 00:02:42.704
[Body thudding]

00:02:42.704 --> 00:02:43.788
- [Crowd:] Ohhh!
- [Announcer:] Oh!

00:02:43.788 --> 00:02:44.789
[Crowd cheering]

00:02:44.789 --> 00:02:46.207
[Buzzer buzzing]

00:02:46.207 --> 00:02:47.750
- [Angela:] It's been
an incredible journey.

00:02:47.750 --> 00:02:50.336
He's always played at a, a high level.

00:02:50.336 --> 00:02:52.630
He's always been very competitive.

00:02:54.007 --> 00:02:55.508
You know, all three, all three children—

00:02:55.508 --> 00:02:56.968
you know, sports in general

00:02:56.968 --> 00:02:58.887
just teaches a lot of life lessons.

00:02:58.887 --> 00:03:00.930
- [Dalen:] My seventh-grade year,

00:03:00.930 --> 00:03:02.682
which was his fourth-grade year,

00:03:02.682 --> 00:03:04.559
we played on the same, like, travel team.

00:03:04.559 --> 00:03:06.269
He was three grades behind me.

00:03:06.269 --> 00:03:10.023
And I saw, like, how he was,
you know, competing with us,

00:03:10.023 --> 00:03:11.024
competing with my peers,

00:03:11.024 --> 00:03:13.610
competing with seventh-
graders at fourth grade.

00:03:13.610 --> 00:03:15.028
So, at that time I knew, like, "Oh no,

00:03:15.028 --> 00:03:16.654
he's pretty good in basketball

00:03:16.654 --> 00:03:18.531
and I think that he could take it far."

00:03:20.909 --> 00:03:21.826
- [Dotson:] Sports in general

00:03:21.826 --> 00:03:23.703
has, has always been big in our family.

00:03:23.703 --> 00:03:27.999
But basketball has kind of
taken the, the main focus.

00:03:27.999 --> 00:03:30.627
My, my sister got into it.

00:03:30.627 --> 00:03:31.711
My brother plays.

00:03:32.629 --> 00:03:34.839
It's just a sport that we all love.

00:03:34.839 --> 00:03:35.715
- [Dalen:] When we were playing one-on-one,

00:03:35.715 --> 00:03:38.343
we would kinda like
visualize us, like, in the NBA.

00:03:38.343 --> 00:03:40.553
So, we would, like, play
against the invisible team

00:03:40.553 --> 00:03:41.804
as if we were an NBA team.

00:03:41.804 --> 00:03:43.973
We would go back to our rooms,

00:03:43.973 --> 00:03:47.852
like, get on the plane, do
interviews between games

00:03:47.852 --> 00:03:50.688
and kind of just like
experience the whole NBA.

00:03:50.688 --> 00:03:52.357
- It's been a long wait,

00:03:52.357 --> 00:03:55.235
so it's going to be a
sense of relief almost.

00:03:55.235 --> 00:03:58.071
Like, "Oh, we've, we,
we're finally here."

00:03:58.071 --> 00:04:01.783
And just knowing where
your child is going to be

00:04:01.783 --> 00:04:03.660
and grow and thrive.

00:04:03.660 --> 00:04:04.869
- [Dotson:] What I learned most was

00:04:04.869 --> 00:04:07.372
control what you can control, you know.

00:04:07.372 --> 00:04:09.249
There's things that are going to be

00:04:09.249 --> 00:04:10.375
above what you can control.

00:04:10.375 --> 00:04:13.044
And as long as you stay
level-headed, stay locked in

00:04:13.044 --> 00:04:15.380
and continue to work at it every day,

00:04:15.380 --> 00:04:17.632
stuff should find its way.

00:04:17.632 --> 00:04:20.760
The amount of work and amount
of dedication that it takes,

00:04:20.760 --> 00:04:23.096
you know, you can't—I mean,
sometimes on the weekends

00:04:23.096 --> 00:04:24.347
you want to go hang out.

00:04:24.347 --> 00:04:26.516
Nah, you got to stay in
the gym, and, you know,

00:04:26.516 --> 00:04:27.850
work overtime. But, you know,

00:04:27.850 --> 00:04:29.102
I wouldn't trade it for anything.

00:04:29.102 --> 00:04:30.228
- [Dalen:] As a big brother,

00:04:30.228 --> 00:04:31.980
seeing my little brother
accomplish all his dreams

00:04:31.980 --> 00:04:33.690
is definitely gonna be a proud feeling.

00:04:33.690 --> 00:04:35.233
Yeah, I'm looking forward to it.

00:04:35.233 --> 00:04:36.985
- You know, it's just been gratifying

00:04:36.985 --> 00:04:39.946
as a dad just to see how
hard he's worked at it

00:04:39.946 --> 00:04:42.115
and the benefits that
he's receiving from it.

00:04:42.115 --> 00:04:44.200
- Just really grateful
for this opportunity,

00:04:44.200 --> 00:04:47.203
and we know he's going
to do, do big things.

00:04:47.203 --> 00:04:49.455
- For him to be able to live his dream

00:04:49.455 --> 00:04:50.957
is not only inspiring,

00:04:50.957 --> 00:04:53.001
but I'm just like genuinely proud for him

00:04:53.001 --> 00:04:53.918
because I know that's
what he's always wanted

00:04:53.918 --> 00:04:55.461
since he was little.

